						<div class='article-twenty-twenty-style-content mb-5 '><p><span class="dropcap">T</span>he key was to make everyone as indebted to you as possible. That’s why I made the dumplings for my Super Smash Sundays from scratch and made sure my guests always saw me folding them by hand. The user journey looked like this: you take off your shoes and put on the pair of animal-themed house slippers I’ve selected for you, grab a glass of home-brewed mugicha from the kitchen, choose your favorite color GameCube controller from the bin, then find your tourney partner in the living room based on whoever had your matching pair of slippers. Between games, you’d mingle with the other guests; even though you’ve always considered yourself an introvert, the conversation would flow easily because I would have made sure you’d have at least three mutual friends in the room. You’d feel like you already knew everyone, that this is where you belong. If it was your first time, I’d take a flattering Polaroid of you and ask you to sign it, then add it to my community wall. Every thirty minutes or so, the steaming plate of fresh dumplings in the play area would be refilled without you having to move an inch – just like at your mom’s house.</p>
<p>My presence would be felt, but not overtly. My regulars always asked me to play in the tourney but I made a show of being too busy clearing plates or refilling drinks. I took on the MC role of deciding the team pairings and matchup sequence, live-updating the tourney bracket and then announcing the winner. This ensured that my guests would subconsciously think of me as both a provider and an authority figure, the benevolent administrator of their social lives.</p>
<p>I knew I had achieved product-market fit for Smash Sundays when white VC bros I barely knew who lived in the Marina started DMing me asking if they could ‘roll through’. <em>Yo – saw [insert founder they were chasing] post about Smash Sundays on Instagram. Looked super lit can I join for the next?? I’ll bring Chinese food!</em> We had a name for these interlopers: Marco Polos, or marcos for short. The marcos sought an audience with the Yuan court of modern San Francisco, the elite inner circle of overwhelmingly male and Asian American AI researchers, engineers, and founders that the entire future of American capitalism seemed to hinge on. The marcos were constantly trying to take advantage of my people and poach them for less than they were worth. That’s why every time they hit me up to join, I declined. Their self-centered motives would taint the spirit of Smash Sundays, which was about trust. My people knew that I was the only one who really had their best interests at heart. That’s why whenever my AI researchers received job offers, they came to me first to help them parse the fine print behind the often eye-wateringly high headline numbers, to make sure they weren’t getting ripped off. That’s why whenever my founders needed to hire AI engineers, they entrusted me to make thoughtful and discreet introductions. I was the honest broker, a role I treated with reverence. My people understood that even though I wasn’t ‘technical’, I was one of them. We were bonded by the shared experience of spending every Sunday in Chinese school, playing <em>MapleStory</em>, watching the same Asian YouTubers, eating Costco pizza, training for Mathcounts/AMC, résumé-padding at a hyper-competitive 50 percent+ Asian public high school, and watching the CCTV New Year’s Gala.</p>
<p>I was proud to see our community take care of each other. Since I started hosting Smash Sundays, my regulars had invited me – unprompted, by the way – to invest in three of the hottest AI deals of the year, which turned me into a rising star at work. That encouraged me to create even more opportunities for the community to come together. I started with Friday poker nights, which were an instant success and got me a foothold into the weekend rotation. After that I secured a recurring pickleball court reservation in the Mission every Saturday morning, followed by afternoon sessions at the indoor climbing gym. This meant I had 2–3× weekly touchpoints with the key SF AI talents, guaranteeing that they saw me more frequently than any other investor in the city. Except for the ten-mile run through the Presidio that I took every Sunday morning to clear my head, I was spending every minute of every weekend tending to my flock.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="dropcap">A</span>t 9 a.m. on Monday my boss Danny, one of the younger partners at the VC fund I worked at, asked me why we missed the Zelion deal.</p>
<p>I stiffened, feeling the hyena eyes of Eric and Kenneth, my two rivals in the associate cohort, boring into my neck from across the bullpen. ‘Are you sure? They only raised their Series A four months ago. I just saw Kelvin last week and he promised he would save an allocation for us.’</p>
<p>‘Pre-empted by Velar Capital. Go figure out what happened and fix it.’</p>
<p>That night I took a Sharpie and scribbled out Kelvin’s face from his Polaroid on the community wall. Velar Capital’s involvement meant only one thing – Colter, the Tribeca-based Golden Boy of Silicon Alley. My hands quivering with anger, I pulled up Colter’s Instagram story and there he was, my so-called friend Kelvin (the CEO of Zelion) playing poker on a Velar yacht in Nassau wearing some ridiculous Kith hoodie custom-embroidered with the Zelion logo that Colter had obviously special-ordered for him. The video ended with Colter turning the camera on selfie mode and doing his lip-bite-fist-pump dance move while EDM blasted in the background. Seething with indignation, I replayed the story, pausing at the frame where a glimpse of Colter’s upper abs rippled out from under his nearly unbuttoned shirt.</p>
<p>Colter was only a few years older than me but had already made general partner at Velar, the high-flying New York hedge fund that had carpet-bagged its way into venture capital after the tech bubble burst. He was one of those Park Avenue frat types who never seemed to take anything seriously then mysteriously appeared on the summa cum laude list at graduation. I’d actually known him during college through a 9 a.m. linear algebra class we had together when he was a senior and I was a freshman. I still had his number saved from when I used to send him pictures of my completed homework psets. He usually found me in the library the night before exams to catch him up on all the material from the lectures he’d skipped. After every late-night study sesh he’d insist we needed to hang out outside of class, ‘grab lunch or something’, but never followed through.</p>
<p>The last time I saw him was at a Penn young alums event in the city two years ago. I’d spent the entire night floating at the edge of the conversational clusters that formed around Colter, but he never seemed to spot me. Finally, as I was standing at the bar waiting to order a drink, Colter walked right over – only to cut in line at the bar as if he couldn’t see me. Then, with two lagers in hand, he accidentally bumped into me and splashed my shirtsleeve with beer. We looked straight at each other; I wiped my sleeve and smiled at him warmly. He scanned my face without a trace of recognition, said a quick ‘my fault my fault’ and went on his way.</p>
<p>Every time I think about that encounter I feel soaked from inside with a tingling, piss-warm indignation. And since then, I’ve become addicted to watching Colter’s Instagram stories, deliberately seeking out that burning tinge of parasocial resentment.</p>
<p>Which is why Colter’s re-entry into my life was so insulting. To encroach, as a <em>marco</em>, on my turf, and have the nerve to shower Kelvin with his attention when he couldn’t even remember my name – I couldn’t tolerate it.</p>
<p>I reminded myself that this was a temporary humiliation. In this business, whoever controlled the talent controlled everything. Next time I’d be the one leading the deal, and when Colter came to me to ask for allocation, I’d make him beg. I tried to ice out my anxiety by taking out my phone and opening up the iMessage app. But instead of Kelvin, I swallowed my pride and texted Rex.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="dropcap">I</span>n Rex’s guest bedroom I changed into the kimono he laid out on the bed and admired his collection of Joseon-era moon jars that seemed to float on the ambiently backlit cypress shelves. On his bedstand were two mini bottles of Fiji water arranged neatly behind the lamp like in a hotel room, a discreet wooden tissue box filled with baby wipes, and a yolk-yellow quart of ‘Boy Butter’ lube.</p>
<p>‘You’re early this month. Did you prep?’</p>
<p>Even though I should be used to it by now, I was always struck by how remarkably tense Rex was. His acute autism made it impossible for him to sustain eye contact, so I locked my gaze on the engorged blue vein splitting his forehead. ‘Yes,’ I lied, in the alluring breathy voice I reserved for such occasions. ‘I just couldn’t wait to see you.’ He had a weird fixation that for the twenty-four hours before every rendezvous I had to cleanse myself by only consuming barley tea and <em>seolleongtang</em>. Eyeing me suspiciously, he lifted my kimono to inspect my chastity cage with feigned disgust and nodded curtly. That was my cue to put on the blindfold.</p>
<p>I’d been seeing Rex (if you could call it that) for about six months now. He was in his early fifties but his Mephistophelian devotion to scientific anti-aging practices had embalmed an unnatural tautness in his skin, which seemed to revolt by taking on a cadaverous gray hue. The one time I jokingly asked Rex if he had a blood boy he’d gotten defensive and changed the topic. Rex was one of the leading men in Silicon Valley. Though our relationship was an unrecognized state, he had shown a promising anger the one time I mentioned going on a date. After that I deleted all my dating apps. Any rumors could result in a blackball and my file had to be clean.</p>
<p>Rex did have a type – the last three boys before me had all been Asian, Korean to be precise, and I was the first Chinese one. Everyone knew this. None of them had lasted more than four months, though all three had got something meaningful out of it in the end. The last boy had even gotten his own small VC fund to run.</p>
<p>After he finished, Rex collapsed onto the bed and lay half prone with his head on my chest. Cautiously, I ran my fingers through his hair and, feeling his breathing slow, timed my opening. I only had a few minutes before the post-coital hormones wore off. This was the Sisyphean game I played every month – how to contrive a reason to linger, ferment intimacy, and thereby alchemize our recurring appointment into a committed relationship.</p>
<p>‘Should we put on a movie? Maybe you can explain <em>Metropolis</em> to me again!’ That was Rex’s favorite movie. He had a habit of replaying random scenes from it while I was over and doom-staring into the screen.</p>
<p>I felt his neck muscles contract. ‘No, it’ll impact my sleep score. I have to be awake in six hours,’ he said, rolling off me to the other side of the bed. I sighed – it was only 9 p.m. Rex and his ilk lived like inverse vampires, rarely awake after dusk.</p>
<p>‘Is something wrong?’</p>
<p>‘You didn’t cum hands-free,’ he said, an accusatory undertone creeping into his voice. He turned back to face me and blinked furiously. Rex blinked a lot when he was upset. ‘Have you been cheating on the cage?’</p>
<p>‘No, of course not! You’re the only one who has the key,’ I pleaded.</p>
<p>‘That’s not what I meant,’ he snapped. My heart lit up with a glimmer of hope. Was he jealous?</p>
<p>‘I couldn’t take more than what you give me, even if it’s just once a month,’ I said coquettishly. Rex shot me a disdainful look.</p>
<p>‘Why do you always make me wear these kimonos anyway?’ I said playfully, trying to change the topic.</p>
<p>I could tell that was the wrong thing to say because it set off a thunderstorm of aggressive blinking.</p>
<p>‘Um, I don’t know what you mean by that. These are hanboks, not kimonos. You thought I was giving you kimonos like some stereotypical <em>otaku</em> creep?’ He pronounced the word ‘otaku’ with a Japanese accent.</p>
<p>‘Of course not!’ I cried. ‘I guess they’re all kind of similar. In China we call them <em>qipao</em>.’</p>
<p>Rex fell silent.</p>
<p>‘What’s wrong?’</p>
<p>‘You’re not Korean?’</p>
<p>I laughed nervously. ‘Of course not silly!’ That was my third time saying it. ‘I’m Chinese, we talked about this!’ I said, even though we hadn’t. In fact, the first time I met Rex, I’d purposely steered us into an hour-long conversation about Korean skincare and the quality of coffee shops in the Seongsu neighborhood of Seoul (I’d never been). That’s when he added me on Telegram. The last time I was over, I’d made <em>samgyetang</em> for him. The mood in the room was quickly souring.</p>
<p>‘Not Korean,’ he repeated. ‘Okay, I see. Go unlock yourself.’ He left the key on the nightstand, retrieved a chilled eucalyptus towel from the towel fridge and placed it next to my naked body. Like the eel at the end of omakase, this was a signal that the experience was coming to an end. But this time, the coldness of Rex’s manner suggested I might not be invited back.</p>
<p>In Rex’s bathroom, I unlocked myself and then, without thinking, hoisted myself onto the corner of the marble vanity top and pressed all my weight down. I held the position for fifteen seconds until I couldn’t bear it any longer.</p>
<p>I returned cupping my crotch with my hands. ‘Daddy, something’s wrong . . . I think I was too hard for you while I was locked up.’</p>
<p>Rex gestured for me to approach his side of the bed and inspected. A moment of disgust flashed his face before melting into something that resembled sympathy.</p>
<p>‘Let’s put some ice on that,’ he said. ‘Here, lie down.’</p>
<p>I nodded cutely, drawing the blanket over myself. Rex returned with an ice pack and a cup of hot cordyceps tea. I sat up just far enough to hold my head level and took small, effortful sips. When I noticed him looking at his phone, I feigned chills and started shivering.</p>
<p>Rex touched the back of his hand against my forehead. ‘You’re hot,’ he murmured. He went to his closet and came back with an oat-colored cashmere hoodie. I put the hoodie on, pulling the sleeves over my hands to make it look oversized. He climbed back into bed with me and wrapped an arm around me awkwardly; I let my head roll onto his chest.</p>
<p>‘What are your plans for the summer?’ Rex asked abruptly.</p>
<p>Though my heart was hammering I continued to ‘play dead’. This was the first time he’d ever mentioned the future. ‘Just a few weddings,’ I lied. ‘What about you?’</p>
<p>‘I’ll be at the chateau in Tahoe,’ he said, running his fingers through my hair. Though the shimmering possibility now hung in the air, I waited for him to take the lead.</p>
<p>‘It’s possible we could arrange to have you visit. I’ll have to check my schedule to see if there’s a weekend that could make sense,’ he said hesitantly.</p>
<p>‘That sounds lovely,’ I said, taking care not to sound too eager. ‘Yes, let me know.’</p>
<p>We stayed like that for a little while longer, anticipation and anxiety rippling through our bodies. Not wanting to overstay my welcome, I told Rex that I called an Uber. This time, he walked me to the door.</p>
<p>‘I’m hosting my annual Lunar New Year party here in February. Would you like to come?’ he said.</p>
<p>‘I’d love to!’ I said, beaming through the ache in my groin. The invitation to Rex’s party arrived via paperless post the next morning, curing me of any lingering gloom from losing the Zelion deal.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="dropcap">I </span>was at Costco scrambling to buy dumpling ingredients when I got a text from Jerry, one of my most prized assets. I’d been cultivating him since he was a broke postdoc at Stanford and my efforts were about to pay off. Jerry was an early employee at OpenBrain who was in the middle of secret talks to start his own AI company. Since the rumors of his departure began circulating last year he’d been hounded by marcos like Colter pressing him with term sheets. As his trusted friend, I’d taken on the role of shepherding him through these preliminary discussions, nudging him toward potential partners that would ally with me.</p>
<p><em>Yo bro cool if my friend Alex Chen from Lucid rolls through tonight for SS?</em></p>
<p><em>Yes for sure!</em> I texted back immediately. Lucid was one of the hottest new research labs in SF, but Jerry’s request was more or less a formality since the answer was always yes – trusted referrals were the lifeblood of my business because the most prized tier of AI technical talent traveled in packs, clustering around tight, impenetrable cohort groups from Google or Stanford. The number of introductions made was a KPI I tracked for every contact in my spreadsheet and on this metric Jerry was an MVN – a Most Valuable Node.</p>
<p>Jerry’s track record was so flawless that I hadn’t even bothered to look up Alex Chen. So imagine my surprise when Jerry sheepishly walked in accompanied by an attractive Asian woman.</p>
<p>‘Ooh, I love your apron! So cute!’ she said, using the annoying ‘sassy’ voice heterosexual women reserve for gay men. ‘I brought you some pastries!’</p>
<p>‘Thanks!’ I said, sizing her up between pleasantries. The first thing I honed in on was the incongruity between her natural attractiveness – based on facial symmetry, skin clarity and physique I guessed she’d probably be around a high eight to low nine on a heterosexual scale – and her stereotypically frumpy tech-girl outfit of Lucid Labs hoodie, leggings, and generic white sneakers. It was unusual for a high eight to low nine to dress like this. At the same time, she was wearing perfect makeup, and I could also detect an assuredness and high social horsepower humming just beneath the surface, which made her unassuming conformist outfit seem like a getup.</p>
<p>I showed Alex her position in the Smash bracket and went to the bathroom to freshen up. When I came back, no one was playing Smash. Jerry and four other engineers had left their controllers on the floor and were clustered around Alex. They were taking turns asking her questions and laughing sycophantically, totally oblivious to Alex’s hardcore social engineering. She was throwing out the most basic ‘Oh my God no way [insert boba chain / video game / anime] is my favorite too!’ and tilting her head while listening and all of it was hitting the targets dead-on, obliterating my friends’ psychological defenses.</p>
<p>I fixed my face into my best corporate grin and set a plate of fresh dumplings down in the middle of the circle.</p>
<p>‘Hi! So what do you work on at Lucid Labs?’ I asked.</p>
<p>‘I was a product manager! So, you know, basically just supporting the brilliant researchers at Lucid and getting out of their way.’ That drew more tickled laughs from her enamored audience.</p>
<p>‘Was?’ I pressed gently, taking care to inflect my tone with curiosity, rather than suspicion.</p>
<p>‘Oh, yes! I left last month actually. I think what I learned at Lucid was my real passion is supporting the people who are building the AI future generally, not just working on one specific project myself, you know?’</p>
<p>‘I see. So you’re taking some time off, then?’</p>
<p>Alex laughed modestly. ‘Oh no! I actually just signed with Sutton Hale, haha – girl’s gotta pay rent!’</p>
<p>This was the first time another venture capitalist had infiltrated Smash Sundays. Besides that, Sutton Hale was a sickeningly prestigious venture-capital firm, one of the most storied names on Sand Hill Road. Rex was a partner there. Hundreds of the most pedigreed analysts tripped over themselves to compete for the two junior spots they had each year. Even the technicals, who usually thought of VCs as talentless hacks, were excited to call their parents to tell them their start-up had raised from Sutton Hale. And here was Alex, acting like it was no big deal.</p>
<p>‘Yeah I know, never really thought I’d join the dark side, but they were really convincing!’ she said with another fake laugh. ‘Anyway, I don’t start until next week. Guess we’ll just see how it goes.’</p>
<p>When Alex revealed that she had just moved from New York City, the energy of the conversation instantly dialed up a notch.</p>
<p>‘No way, I have to show you the best climbing gym . . .’</p>
<p>‘. . . the best matcha cafe.’</p>
<p>The night dragged on. Just as I started my solo cleanup ritual, Alex popped into the kitchen to help. I bristled when she casually defied my instructions to let it be and started taking Tupperware out of my cabinets and deftly packing the leftovers. The regulars looked on in awe.</p>
<p>‘Hey Miles, can you add Alex to the group chat?’ Jerry said as he was putting on his shoes in the hallway.</p>
<p>‘Yeah man, add her!’ Jerry’s friend Kevin echoed.</p>
<p>‘Of course!’ I said, amping my corporate grin up to its maximum brightness. ‘I’ll add her right away! Goodnight everyone!’</p>
<p>Alex’s LinkedIn raised some questions – she’d deliberately removed the months at Lucid, a classic tactic to cover up a short stint. Her longest employment before Lucid was McKinsey and she’d attended Dartmouth for undergrad – red flags. I switched to Instagram for evidence of sorority involvement or dating white guys. She didn’t have any pictures before 2023; everything had been posted in the last two years, zero tagged photos. Her entire grid was dominated by pictures of baking, hiking, matcha, and traveling. The only party picture was her at EDC with a group of all-Asian, approachable-looking sixes or sevens. There wasn’t a single male in any of her photos. She was available, down to earth; in other words, she was the perfect girlfriend for an AI researcher, but it was a thin wrapper, just enough to trip over the uncanny valley. I’d seen enough to know something was amiss.</p>
<p>Everyone kept texting me to ask if Alex was coming to Sunday Smash. Attendance reached an all-time high, but nobody seemed interested in playing Smash anymore. Worse still, she started showing up at my Friday poker and Saturday climbing kickbacks too. Like a shitty expense management software billboard ad, suddenly she was everywhere.</p>
<p>Then, three weeks after she arrived, the cancellations started coming in. People who never missed a Smash Sunday for six months in a row were suddenly tied up. Finally, an Instagram story confirmed what I already knew: they were all at Alex’s place in Haight-Ashbury.</p>
<p>I sent a text to the Sunday Smash group chat: <em>Hi everyone!! So my upstairs neighbor flooded their place and it’s leaking into mine . . . unfortunately need to cancel tonight &#x2639;</em></p>
<p>That got a bunch of heart reactions and replies like <em>Oh no!! Hope everything’s alright.</em></p>
<p>Next, I screenshotted the Instagram story and got ChatGPT to give me the coordinates. Since the party was in the backyard, I just snuck in through the garden gate. Everyone was Asian and all my regulars were there: Jerry with his OpenBrain entourage, Brian and Jason with the Substrate/Monad Labs squads. Even that traitor Kelvin, wearing the same streetwear hoodie I saw in Colter’s Instagram story. I picked up a hard yuzu seltzer from the cooler and began a slow lap. Inserting myself into a conversation should’ve been easy, considering I’d hosted everyone more than a dozen times. But everyone kept giving me these darting and unsettled looks, as if they were embarrassed to see me there, and whenever I brushed up against a circle, it seemed to clench up, its members pointing their feet closer to the imaginary center and turning their backs stiffly on me.</p>
<p>Beaming, Alex walked out of the kitchen wearing a pink apron with a huge, steaming plate of dumplings. She set them down on the picnic table while everyone cheered.</p>
<p>Joining the queue, I seethed as I eavesdropped on the compliments.</p>
<p>‘<em>Honestly I come to these just for the dumplings</em>.’</p>
<p>‘SO good. Low-key the best dumplings I’ve ever had.’</p>
<p>At the front of the line, Alex was holding court and insincerely thanking people for coming. To my amazement, she pretended not to see me. My hand quivering, I picked one of the dumplings up from the plate and plopped it into my mouth. I recognized the taste – they were frozen Wei-Chuan dumplings from 99 Ranch Market. Soaked with indignation, I stood rooted to the spot, chewing slowly, while people behind me in the line shuffled their feet.</p>
<p>‘Hey dude, are you okay?’ It was Jerry, standing next to me with a concerned look on his face. ‘You’re sweating.’</p>
<p>‘I’m fine. Just need to freshen up,’ I snapped, disappearing into the house. I tracked through the hallways silently until I found Alex’s bedroom. I ran my fingertips across her desktop, bookshelf, and nightstand. Then I took a seat on her bed, one of those 45,000 water-controlled smart mattresses tech freaks use to optimize their sleep performance, and contemplated the room. The barren walls, the meticulously made bed, the complete absence of dust or debris – it was the type of living space only an unhinged psychopath could construct. After a moment of hesitation, I tapped the touchpad on her MacBook and found it unlocked. I opened her Notes app and started to read.</p>
<blockquote><p>‘Gratitude Journal 7.2.25’<br />
<em>today grateful for:</em><br />
<em>kind peeps who made me feel welcome in a new city</em><br />
<em>biweekly facetimes with my ny girlies</em><br />
<em>living walking distance from H Mart</em><br />
<em>ceremonial grade matcha</em><br />
<em>clean air</em><br />
<em>new beginnings</em></p></blockquote>
<p>I frowned, feeling gaslit, as if she had laid out this implausibly vapid note for me to find on purpose. I was about to read further when I heard voices, so I slipped out of Alex’s bedroom into her en suite. Then I pulled my cock out and started urinating on the floor. I was enthralled by the force of the hydraulic pressure stored in my bladder, the thrust and flow rate of my piss surging forth in a steaming, vindictive jet. As I rotated midstream to splash Alex’s vanity, her electric toothbrush, the mirror, and her towels, a gleeful cackle escaped from somewhere deep inside me. I was still wearing that deranged grin when I opened the door and found Kelvin waiting. I stammered something incomprehensible before speeding past him into the hallway, down the stairs, and out into the street.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="dropcap">Y</span>ear-end reviews were starting in two weeks, which is when they were going to tell us which of the three associates would be promoted to partner. My former winning streak had put me in the lead, but after the Zelion mishap I couldn’t afford any more mistakes.</p>
<p>Something about the snide tone in Kenneth’s voice during our pipeline meeting that morning had put me on edge. Then at noon I got the push notification on my phone: Sutton Hale was leading the seed round for Jerry’s new company, Mantle Labs. I immediately recognized the picture they used of Jerry and his co-founder, which I had taken for them at my apartment’s co-working lounge. The post already had thirty likes on LinkedIn, including Brian, Jason, and Kevin, as well as several reposts, which kept it at the top of my feed every time I refreshed. Jerry had publicly humiliated me with a quote praising Alex as ‘an early believer and an amazing mentor’. After Danny finished viciously dressing me down at my cubicle, I slid into a conference room and called Jerry.</p>
<p>‘I’m sorry, man. I know how disappointed you must be,’ Jerry said, his voice sounding stilted and rehearsed, like one of those pre-recorded phone trees.</p>
<p>‘Disappointed?’ I said. ‘How many times did I make dinner for you and your friends? Drive you to Costco just because you wanted food-court hot dogs? Edit and caption your Coffee Meets Bagel photos? Promote your DJ sets on my story? Teach you how to use the post office?’ I was reading off my spreadsheet and suddenly noticed that I was yelling. ‘And <em>this</em> is how you treat me?’</p>
<p>‘Woah, slow down. I thought the reason you did all that stuff was because you were my friend.’</p>
<p>‘Of <em>course</em> that’s why I did them!’ I snapped.</p>
<p>‘I hope everything is okay,’ he continued shakily. ‘Honestly, I’ve been hearing some pretty weird things about you bro. Between us, I did ask Alex if we could leave some allocation for you in the deal. And she said no. I tried, Miles. I’m sorry.’</p>
<p>I was just about to ask Jerry exactly what he’d heard when he hung up.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span class="dropcap">T</span>he doorman checked my name off the list and brought me to the backyard where the party was being held. Rex’s backyard extended to the vanishing edge of the steep cliff overlooking China Beach and you could see the silhouette of the Marin Headlands through the fog. For his event, Rex had turned the space into a fanciful imperial Chinese garden. On the patio, staff in crisp white uniforms carved Peking ducks and served cocktails at a red lacquer bar. Connecting the patio to the lawn was a garden bridge astride a pond where half a dozen jumbo koi cruised lazy figure eights. From there you took a footpath, illuminated by red-paper lanterns, to a huge red-and-gold party tent made to look like a Chinese pavilion.</p>
<p>I spotted Rex at the cocktail table under the pavilion. He was wearing a purple hanbok and stiffly holding court with a few members of his peerage – the senior VC partners that constituted the merchant-nobility of Menlo-Atherton – and together they formed an impenetrable, cashmere-wrapped circle. Fanning out from the center table, the founders of OpenBrain, Substrate, and Monad Labs were munching duck pancakes with some of the world’s leading AI researchers. This was the most valuable cluster in the entire network. I took a deep gulp of my yuzu seltzer and approached. I felt the approving gaze of Rex’s guests and found myself standing taller. I imagined the thrilling possibility of becoming familiar to these people, someone they thought of affectionately, shared inside jokes with, invited over for dinner. It was possible, as long as I made sure Rex invited me to Lake Tahoe.</p>
<p>My euphoria was interrupted by a nagging awareness of two heads of black hair bobbing in the golden company. They were two slender Korean men approximately my height and weight whom I recognized as Rex’s Tuesday and Thursday boys from last year; the latter I had only recently displaced (fake Grindr screenshots) over the summer. We were all wearing very similar outfits and keeping an eye on each other while refusing to make eye contact. They were both loitering just a few paces away from Rex’s table, clearly angling for an opportunity to speak to our host first, which I obviously could not allow, and when Rex left his table, I intercepted him.</p>
<p>For a split second it seemed like he was walking faster to make it inside the house without saying hello. But I shouted his name loud enough that he had to turn around. He gave me a once-over and recoiled slightly.</p>
<p>‘Happy new year Rex! I brought you some <em>maehwa</em> flowers,’ I said, using the Korean word for plum blossom and presenting the seasonally appropriate bouquet. It was probably my imagination but I thought I saw a twinge of annoyance when I said the word <em>maehwa</em>.</p>
<p>‘Hi, yes, glad you could make it,’ Rex said, without uncrossing his arms. ‘And thank you for the flowers. Why don’t you go put those in the kitchen?’</p>
<p>I couldn’t let him go that easily. ‘The color matches your hanbok,’ I continued winsomely, the bouquet still hanging in the air between us. Then I shifted my expression to a more flirtatious register. ‘Isn’t it funny how this time <em>I’m</em> wearing the suit and <em>you’re</em> wearing the hanbok? Usually it’s the other way around!’</p>
<p>Rex’s face was overtaken by an uncontrolled bout of blinking. I winced.</p>
<p>‘Wait,’ I said, lowering the bouquet to my side. ‘I’m sorry – you look stressed. Is there anything for the party I can help you with? I mean, if it would help you relax, I did prep for you before this –’</p>
<p>‘<em>Stop</em> that,’ Rex hissed. Now he looked almost startled, as if he was scared of what I was about to say, or scared to overhear himself participating in our usual exchange. He squeezed his eyes shut but when he opened them again, he was beaming.</p>
<p>‘Alex!’</p>
<p>My body seized up. Alex had secured an invite when none of Rex’s other junior employees were included. She’d shed her frumpy tech-girl disguise and squeezed herself into a silky red <em>qipao</em> that exaggerated her hip to waist ratio and flaunted her toned arms. As I watched them warmly greet one another, I felt threatened by their rapport, the way they whispered excitedly to each other about something as if I wasn’t still standing right there, holding the <em>maehwa</em> bouquet. Then Alex turned toward me and gave me this super-condescending, apologetic smile – <em>oh I’m sorry for excluding you, just one moment</em> – and without saying goodbye, they walked side by side down the footpath.</p>
<p>Rex clinked his glass with a spoon and the partygoers gathered on the lawn below turned their attention to him. He started on a stilted speech about the significance of lunar new year and the contribution of Asian Americans to the tech industry. I was barely listening, focusing instead on wearing my warmest, most admiring smile at the front of the crowd where Rex could see me. But then he started thanking Alex for planning this year’s party and the crowd cheered as Alex took her place next to Rex and she gave a shy little bow. Lurking below the pavilion deck, I felt the propitious future I’d seen so clearly when I arrived dissipate.</p>
<p>After the applause died down, Alex was still lingering onstage. Rex went on for another minute about Alex’s ‘preternatural ability to connect with the world’s best AI talent’ before announcing that starting next week, she would be Sutton Hale’s youngest partner.</p>
<p>I choked down a bitter laugh. There was that piss-warm feeling of indignation again.</p>
<p>Rex told everyone to enjoy themselves, and that in a few minutes a well-known dance troupe from Hong Kong was going to perform their lion dance on the pavilion. Then for the finale of the evening, Alex would light the fireworks.</p>
<p>The crowd dispersed across the lawn while I paced back to the patio to compose myself. But as soon as I got there, the sliding door opened and Colter walked out from the house.</p>
<p>He was wearing a dark suit, a couple of the buttons on his white shirt were undone and he was already drunk, clearly having come from another event. After scanning the crowd, Colter rolled up to the red lacquer bar and ordered two negronis. After fixing my hair, I positioned myself two feet away from him and willed him to say hello. But then he leaned his elbow against the counter to check his phone so that his back was facing me. The bartender placed the drinks on the counter. As Colter reached across to grab them, I pressed my body against his so that when he turned we collided with each other. The splash left a satisfying ruby stain on my white shirt.</p>
<p>‘Oh shit, I’m so sorry, bro!’ Colter cried, his eyes flicking between my face and the red stain on my shirt.</p>
<p><em>You’re so fine! Wait, didn’t we take a class together in college? Your name is Colter, right?</em></p>
<p><em>All good bro. Colter, right? Nice work on Zelion, by the way – Kelvin is a close friend.</em></p>
<p><em>Déjà vu! Haha remember when this exact sequence happened last time? How crazy is that!</em></p>
<p>But before I could get any words out, he’d mouthed a ‘my fault bro’ and slipped away.</p>
<p>I watched Colter saunter over the garden bridge toward Alex, who was holding court with a circle of admirers. Smirking, he interrupted their conversation, handed Alex the other negroni, and raised his glass to ‘the competition getting hotter’. Alex rolled her eyes and they clinked their glasses together. I watched as Colter launched into a whole routine, practically groveling to get a laugh out of Alex, until I couldn’t take it anymore.</p>
<p>I whipped my phone out and took a zoomed-in picture of Colter whispering in Alex’s ear.</p>
<p>The caption sparked out from my fingertips like electricity. ‘Who could’ve guessed everyone’s favorite golden child is just another MARCO worshipper!!’ I sent the picture to the group chat confidently, waiting for the heart and haha reacts to roll in. A few seconds later, my phone began to buzz.</p>
<p><em>&lt;Chris Chan has left the group&gt;</em></p>
<p><em>&lt;Jerry Zhou has left the group&gt;</em></p>
<p><em>&lt;Eric Qiu has left the group&gt;</em></p>
<p><em>&lt;Peter Song has left the group&gt;</em></p>
<p>I stood alone in the gloam, numb to everything except the haptic feedback from my phone pulsing in my palm. At that exact moment, Rex started tapping his glass again to gather the partygoers.</p>
<p>The crowd applauded and took their seats facing the pavilion, Alex next to Rex, his peerage, and Colter in the front row. While the lion dancers performed, I retreated to the patio bar and got another yuzu seltzer. The only people in this part of the backyard were myself and a drunk guest who was tossing leftover dumplings to the jumbo koi. This sent the koi into a feeding frenzy; they leapt and thrashed against each other to snatch the dumplings, splashing big waves onto the patio. I stared at the back of Rex’s and Alex’s heads and wondered whether I should hurl myself over the cliff behind the pavilion to ruin the party. Instead, while everyone was watching the lion dancers, I made my way over to the firework setup and poured what was left of the yuzu seltzer down the mortar funnel.</p>
<p>After the dancers cleared the stage, the event staff moved the fireworks to a grassy area next to the pavilion and invited Alex to come up while Rex looked over her shoulder like a proud parent. I felt almost giddy with excitement.</p>
<p>‘Oh goodness, I’m kinda nervous,’ Alex said, which drew a couple laughs. She crouched down by the base of the mortar and squeezed the barbecue lighter next to the fuse. On her third try, the fuse ignited – the crowd cheered as Alex dashed back to safety and the spark ate its way down the fuse and disappeared into the mortar shaft. Alex was performatively covering her ears and ten, then twenty long seconds passed, but nothing happened. The silence became awkward as we all stood there staring at the dead firework.</p>
<p>‘Sorry, that’s really weird!’ Alex stammered. As I watched her flounder, a cold satisfaction seeped through my entire body.</p>
<p>‘No worries, let’s move the party inside for now,’ Rex said in an unrecognizably gentle voice.</p>
<p>‘No! Ah, sorry let me just check,’ Alex said. Before anyone could stop her, Alex crouched down beside the firework, inspecting it with her face directly next to the mortar. Even as I felt my body instinctively backing up, a queasy but thrilling anticipation locked me in place – I couldn’t look away.</p>
<p>‘I think it’s dead!’ Alex said, looking up with an embarrassed smile. Then there was a flash and a terrible wet bang.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
